  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "claim_7a3cb4e7c8ac45778427464456694a26",
      "statement": "The chapter establishes that the return covariance matrix is not constant over time, motivated by conditional heteroskedasticity (time-dependent variance) and volatility clustering, which make a static sample covariance inadequate and require conditional estimators.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/18_dynamic_covariance_estimation_ewma_and_multivariate_garch.md#L16-L20",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:687732bbf991b28c6fe2a4af931563f640d18c171253e75ffbe8f216b457da9b:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_9862c2ee0f8844329ce58df0b0b635bb",
      "statement": "The RiskMetrics EWMA forecasts conditional variance with the recursion σ²_{t+1|t} = λ·σ²_{t|t-1} + (1-λ)·r²_t, an exponentially weighted sum of past squared returns that reacts faster to shocks than a simple moving average and then decays exponentially.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/18_dynamic_covariance_estimation_ewma_and_multivariate_garch.md#L24-L32",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:b444151baea4ac4f9a2164a73faa0782a300c2fe7d97bd2abe3df2ce4af73c07:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/18_dynamic_covariance_estimation_ewma_and_multivariate_garch.md#L34-L40",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:c10d03fedfc2bcb773fb2265e775ba88a1fa5de22a973345c76b68d9888e8f10:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_7802558060e84231af3983229bea98e4",
      "statement": "EWMA covariance uses the same recursion with the cross-product r_{1,t}·r_{2,t} in place of a single squared return, and the conditional correlation is that covariance divided by the product of the conditional standard deviations.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/18_dynamic_covariance_estimation_ewma_and_multivariate_garch.md#L42-L48",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:2e8e66c3949ee542fb1b4e29c876fe78e1c08523609f7c11af0b735cb45cfec8:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_0c03e00a022145539d09989034dde754",
      "statement": "The EWMA effective sample length is K = ln(Υ_L)/ln(λ), so a λ closer to one uses more observations; at λ=0.97 with 1% tolerance the estimator uses about 151 days of data.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/18_dynamic_covariance_estimation_ewma_and_multivariate_garch.md#L54-L58",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:d4ff495a993da3c0fb7ee3c39211c7aae3b1c1364e13134903c13052099d0181:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_1511dcdd4cb6469b9eefe620ec60c7b0",
      "statement": "Because element-specific decay factors would violate covariance-matrix validity and induce distortion, RiskMetrics applies a single optimal decay factor (minimizing forecast RMSE) to the whole covariance matrix, obtaining 0.94 for daily data and 0.97 for monthly data as an accuracy-weighted average over 450+ series.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/18_dynamic_covariance_estimation_ewma_and_multivariate_garch.md#L60-L70",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:d8b717c96b76caddf8ec317a830c942dbc325afc8837d15ac2a2f0dbb6b476ce:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_157b02556bfb4eba8ac415e83b8011ab",
      "statement": "EWMA multi-step variance follows the square-root-of-time rule σ_{t+T|t} = √T·σ_{t+1|t} with horizon-invariant forecast correlation, and this implies EWMA is essentially an IGARCH (Integrated GARCH) without intercept, i.e. a non-stationary variance process.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/18_dynamic_covariance_estimation_ewma_and_multivariate_garch.md#L74-L80",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:e42f3367dce0be3698107ef8ce2a790a4fafaf4ec094f0c070b2a718ce84450f:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_5dda073dc1d64d06be3bc78bb1435c4f",
      "statement": "The general multivariate GARCH (vec model, and its positive-definite BEKK restriction) suffers a parameter explosion — O(k⁴) parameters in general, reduced to O(k²) in diagonal/scalar form — which limits practical use to few assets; variance targeting mitigates this by fixing the long-run covariance to the sample covariance.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/18_dynamic_covariance_estimation_ewma_and_multivariate_garch.md#L90-L94",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:5efde02ccc469251c5c9a7d5e0127b389eb6c830848a985a4d29896fc0d99a4f:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_5a1a9114955043eb9f08a8189753f53d",
      "statement": "The DCC model decomposes the conditional covariance as H_t = D_t·R_t·D_t, where D_t is the diagonal of univariate-GARCH conditional standard deviations and R_t is the conditional correlation matrix, which equals the conditional covariance of the standardized residuals ε_t = D_t⁻¹·r_t.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/18_dynamic_covariance_estimation_ewma_and_multivariate_garch.md#L100-L104",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:afe11f6b569e2c1f38cd4016d247db6995233cbde82eda5d1a0fd504ba570b06:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_2d723ee023f14c02908c1b838061e5c6",
      "statement": "DCC drives correlation dynamics through an auxiliary matrix Q_t updated by either an integrated exponential smoother or a mean-reverting GARCH(1,1) form (mean-reverting while α+β<1), then rescales Q_t to a valid correlation matrix via R_t = Q*⁻¹·Q_t·Q*⁻¹ with Q* the diagonal of sqrt of Q_t's diagonal.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/18_dynamic_covariance_estimation_ewma_and_multivariate_garch.md#L112-L128",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:b09861a8abd9b6fbd67fdeb234d4baffdd6c48b032de1270ea82c6d63ebc61dd:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_ac53501eafd44011821adc8b397399d2",
      "statement": "DCC positive definiteness holds under the same conditions as a univariate GARCH process (α_m,β_n ≥ 0, Σα_m+Σβ_n < 1, positive minimum eigenvalue of the unconditional correlation), and these conditions are sufficient but not necessary.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/18_dynamic_covariance_estimation_ewma_and_multivariate_garch.md#L136-L136",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:37c4ca9b62a40762fbdd20caa6e4554f31a7d25b0893ea16ef4e3468149ee613:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_34e4d4528d244ae6bde96ee8fbfb9dc4",
      "statement": "DCC's key advantage is that the number of correlation parameters is independent of the number of series, achieved via a likelihood that splits as L(θ,φ)=L_V(θ)+L_C(θ,φ) and a two-step estimator: first fit univariate GARCH variances, then estimate correlation dynamics from the standardized residuals.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/18_dynamic_covariance_estimation_ewma_and_multivariate_garch.md#L140-L158",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:c6d67ffd3188d7756599af1f718998b8844f600b01b91e1073d919b158a0e17d:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_22a7c9956d5b4397a5c110c7d35ec0da",
      "statement": "The DCC two-step estimator is consistent and asymptotically normal with a quasi-maximum-likelihood interpretation (first-stage standard errors coinciding with the Bollerslev–Wooldridge robust estimator), and the Engle–Sheppard constant-correlation test rejects constant correlation in every model considered.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/18_dynamic_covariance_estimation_ewma_and_multivariate_garch.md#L160-L164",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:2bbbf00af707318ebb2ea7f7cfc5ff9166196b754637cf50ac6c33053a9d78e9:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_065962f9aac544a8a72a49715a5c01d7",
      "statement": "Empirically the mean-reverting ML-estimated DCC has the smallest mean absolute error in most Monte Carlo cases, and on the minimum-variance-portfolio specification test it keeps standardized-residual variances inside the confidence band where the RiskMetrics smoother does not.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/18_dynamic_covariance_estimation_ewma_and_multivariate_garch.md#L168-L176",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:0f442c9633cf72c670dac618b05b484e43a72d9984425155211320ae1893e066:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_06acbe5832e34700bf903acb90e157b7",
      "statement": "Dynamic conditional covariance is a direct alternative input to the static sample covariance for optimization: the minimum-variance weights w_t = H_t⁻¹ι/(ι'H_t⁻¹ι) depend entirely on the estimated H_t, so substituting a dynamic forecast makes the optimal weights time-varying and places covariance estimation upstream of the portfolio-optimization problems.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/18_dynamic_covariance_estimation_ewma_and_multivariate_garch.md#L180-L184",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:952daae5d258cf60ef2176c90dce58805d9ccf6afdab785dd2f11687a920faa3:"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
